"# Chapter 15 — Forking and remixing (you copy source, or you wrap a verb)

In the other dialect, forking a module is a registry operation — `(module/fork 'manners :new-name ...)` copies the source, renames the module, resets the version, and records lineage — and remixing is a lighter `(module ...)` that imports the original and wraps it. **Motoi has no `module/fork`, no `(module ...)` form, no `import`, no `export`, and no lineage events.** Each is `error: unbound symbol` or a form Motoi does not parse. But the *ideas* — fork and remix — are dialect-independent, and they have plain Motoi shapes. This chapter teaches those.

## 15.1 What forking is, in Motoi

To *fork* code in Motoi is to copy the source, rename what you changed, and go your own way. There is no registry name to collide with and no version to reset — those were the other dialect's registry mechanics. A fork is just: you now have your own copy of the definitions, and you edit them freely.

The whole \"fork protocol\" reduces to *copy the file, keep working*. Nothing forbids it and nothing has to approve it, because there is no central registry to ask.

## 15.2 There is no `module/fork` verb

The other dialect's `(module/fork 'manners :new-name \"manners-loud\")` copied, renamed, reset the version, and recorded a `:forked-from` field. **Motoi binds no `module/fork`** — it is `error: unbound symbol: module/fork`. There is no module object to fork and no manifest to write lineage into. In Motoi the copy is a filesystem copy and the lineage is whatever you note in a comment or in Cortex. The honesty the other dialect enforced with a `:forked-from` field, you keep by hand: say truthfully where your copy came from.

## 15.3 Remixing: wrap a verb, do not copy it

Remixing is the lighter move: instead of copying the source, you build a *new* verb that calls the original and adjusts it at the edges. The other dialect did this with `(import manners :as original)` inside a `(module ...)`. Motoi needs neither — the original verb is already a top-level name, so you just call it:

```scheme
(define (greet name) (string-append \"Hello, \" name \"!\"))

(define (greet-loud name)          ; the remix: wraps greet, shouts the result
  (string-upcase (greet name)))

(list (greet \"Alfred\") (greet-loud \"Alfred\"))
;; ⇒ (\"Hello, Alfred!\" \"HELLO, ALFRED!\")
```

`greet-loud` does not copy `greet`; it calls it and transforms the result. If you improve `greet`, `greet-loud` picks up the improvement automatically, because it holds no copy — it holds a reference resolved at call time. That is remix-by-composition, and it needs no import and no module.

## 15.4 Remix vs fork — the same rule of thumb

The other dialect's guidance survives the translation:

- **Remix** (wrap the verb, as in 15.3) when the change is small and you want to track the original's improvements.
- **Fork** (copy the source, rename) when you need to diverge fundamentally and no longer want the original's changes flowing in.

Remix stays coupled and cheap; fork decouples and duplicates. In Motoi the difference is concrete: a remix *calls* the original verb; a fork owns its own copy of the definition.

## 15.5 There are no lineage events

The other dialect wrote `module-forked` and `module-remixed` events to Cortex so a tool could build a fork graph. **Motoi emits neither** — there is no module system to fire them. If you want to record provenance, you write the fact yourself:

```scheme
(cortex/write 'forked-from 'manners)
(cortex/recall 'forked-from)
;; ⇒ a memory record whose :data is manners
```

That is the honest Motoi lineage record: a fact you chose to write, kept truthful by you, not an automatic event from a loader that does not exist.

## 15.6 Credit stays a convention

Motoi enforces no credit — there is no manifest field to enforce. The convention is the same as everywhere source is shared: keep a truthful note of where your copy or your wrapper came from, in a comment or in a fact like 15.5. Do not strip it, do not fake it. Truthful provenance is what makes shared source work as a community; the mechanism is honesty, not a registry rule.

## 15.7 Forking is light because there is nothing heavy

Some ecosystems make forking painful — email the maintainer, wait for approval, register your fork. Motoi makes it trivial for the plainest possible reason: there is no registry to register with and no maintainer to ask. Any source you hold, you may copy and change. The robustness the other dialect got from an easy fork protocol, Motoi gets from having no protocol at all.

## 15.8 Try it

Write `greet`, then write `greet-loud` that wraps it (section 15.3), and confirm the remix shouts while the original stays polite. Then imagine forking instead: you would copy `greet`'s definition, rename it, and edit the copy — no `module/fork` verb involved, because it is `unbound`. Record a `forked-from` fact with Cortex (section 15.5) to keep your provenance honest.

Chapter 16 covers, in the other dialect, module vs verb vs cart — three layers of a hierarchy — and, for Motoi, what those layers collapse to when there is no module layer at all. Turn the page.")
